There is a positive definite $k\times k$ matrix $B$ such that the graph matrix has the expansions $$\begin{aligned}
 A(t)&=-\frac1{t-D}
 \begin{pmatrix}B&0\\0&0\end{pmatrix}+R_D(t),
 &&\text{near }D,\label{eq:lag-middle-pole}\\
 A(t)&=-\frac1{t-t_0}\mathrm{Id}_d+R_{t_0}(t),
 &&\text{near }t_0=0,T,\label{eq:lag-end-poles}
\end{aligned}$$ where each remainder extends smoothly across its indicated point. In particular, $C$ and $H$ extend smoothly across $D$, and $H'(D)>0$.

Define $$\begin{aligned}
 h(t)&=\det(H'(t))^{1/m} &&(0<t<T),\\
 s(t)&=\det(S(t))^{1/k} &&(t\in\mathcal I),\\
 s_i(t)&=s(t+iD) &&(0<t<D,\ i=0,1).
 \end{aligned}
 \label{eq:lag-profiles}$$ The function $h$ is smooth and positive across $D$. There are positive constants $c_H,C_H,c_i,C_i$, depending on the fixed geodesic and its chart, such that $$\begin{aligned}
 c_H&\le h(t)\sin^2t\le C_H &&(0<t<T),
 \label{eq:lag-h-bounds}\\
 c_i&\le \frac{s_i(t)\sin^2(2t)}4\le C_i
 &&(0<t<D,\ i=0,1).
 \label{eq:lag-s-bounds}
\end{aligned}$$ Equivalently, the logarithms of the ratios of $h$ to $1/\sin^2t$ and of $s_i$ to $4/\sin^2(2t)$ are bounded.

With the profiles of Proposition <a href="#lag:profiles" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lag:profiles">8</a>, every pair of distinct chart endpoints satisfies $$f_H(x,y)^{1/m}\ge
 \frac{\left|\int_x^y h(t)\,dt\right|}{\sqrt{h(x)h(y)}}.
 \label{eq:lag-H-scalar-bound}$$ For $0<u<v<D$ and $i,j\in\{0,1\}$ one has $$f_K(u+iD,v+jD)^{1/k}\ge
 \frac{
  \left(\int_u^v s_i(t)\,dt\right)^{1/2}
  \left(\int_u^v s_j(t)\,dt\right)^{1/2}}
 {\sqrt{s_i(u)s_j(v)}}.
 \label{eq:lag-K-scalar-bound}$$ Together with endpoint exchange, these bounds cover the full square $(0,T)^2$ except the chart, diagonal, and cut lines, a set of planar measure zero.

Let $b>0$, $\lambda=\pi/b$, and $$p_0(t)=\frac{\lambda^2}{\sin^2(\lambda t)},\qquad 0<t<b.$$ Let $p:(0,b)\to(0,\infty)$ be continuous and suppose that $g=\log(p/p_0)$ is bounded. Let $W$ be nonnegative and measurable on $(0,b)$, with $W(b-z)=W(z)$ almost everywhere and $W(z)/\sin(\lambda z)$ essentially bounded. Write $$\Delta_b=\{(x,y):0<x<y<b\},\qquad
 d\nu_{xy}(t)=\frac{p_0(t)\,dt}{\int_x^y p_0},\quad x<t<y.$$ Then all the following integrals are finite, and $$\begin{aligned}
 &\int_{\Delta_b}W(y-x)
 \left[\log\frac{\int_x^y p}{\int_x^y p_0}
       -\frac{g(x)+g(y)}2\right]dx\,dy \notag\\
 &\qquad=\int_{\Delta_b}W(y-x)
 \left[\log\int_x^y e^{g(t)}\,d\nu_{xy}(t)
       -\int_x^y g(t)\,d\nu_{xy}(t)\right]dx\,dy
 \ \geq\ 0.\label{weighted:scalar-identity}
\end{aligned}$$

The total Pontryagin class of $M$ is $$\label{inc:pontryagin-formula}
 p(TM)=\frac{(1+2su+u^2)^{r+1}}{1+4su}
 \quad\text{in }H^*(M;\mathbb Q).$$ The quotient means its power-series expansion truncated by $u^{r+1}=0$.

With this orientation and Euler class, $$\label{geo:contact-formula}
 N:=\int_B e^{4r-1}
   =\frac{(4r-1)!\,|S^{4r-1}|\,\mathop{\mathrm{Vol}}(M)}{T^{4r}}>0.$$ For the standard quaternionic model at the same diameter, this number is $$\label{geo:model-number}
 N_0=\frac{1}{2r+1}\binom{4r}{2r}.$$

The manifolds $U$ and $B$ are simply connected. There is an integral class $v\in H^4(B;\mathbb Z)$ with $\rho^*v=z$. For $2j<n$, the following is an integral basis: $$\label{geo:low-basis}
 H^{2j}(B;\mathbb Z):\qquad
 e^{j-2a}v^a,\qquad 0\le a\le\lfloor j/2\rfloor.$$ All odd cohomology of $B$ vanishes, and its even cohomology is free abelian. In particular $H^2(B;\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Ze$ and $H^4(B;\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Ze^2\oplus\mathbb Zv$. The rational cohomology ring is generated by $e,v$, and $$\label{geo:total-betti}
 \dim_{\mathbb Q}H^*(B;\mathbb Q)=2r(r+1).$$*



*Multiplication by $e$ at the middle fits into an exact sequence $$\label{geo:middle-sequence}
 0\longrightarrow H^{n-2}(B;\mathbb Z)
 \xrightarrow{\ e\smile\ }H^n(B;\mathbb Z)
 \longrightarrow\mathbb Z/(r+1)\longrightarrow0.$$ Consequently the integral bilinear form $$\label{geo:middle-form}
 (a,b)\longmapsto\int_B eab,\qquad a,b\in H^{n-2}(B;\mathbb Z),$$ has determinant of absolute value $r+1$ in any integral basis. The map $e\smile:H^n(B;\mathbb Z)\to H^{n+2}(B;\mathbb Z)$ is an isomorphism.

There is an oriented rank-three vector bundle $E\to B$ and an attaching map $$f:S(E)\longrightarrow M.$$ Let $p:S(E)\to B$ and put $x=f^*u\in H^4(S(E);\mathbb Z)$. The following properties hold:*

1.  *The pullback $\rho^*E$ is the first negative bundle for the free-loop energy. It is isomorphic as a real vector bundle to the rank-three bundle $K\to U$ with fiber $$K_w=\{a\in w^\perp:
              J_a(D)=0\},
           \quad J_a(0)=0,\quad J_a'(0)=a,$$ where $J_a$ is the normal Jacobi field along $\gamma_w$.*


2.  *Let $\widetilde p:S(\rho^*E)\to U$ and $\widehat\rho:S(\rho^*E)\to S(E)$ be the pullback square. Then $$\label{geo:attachment-pullback}
           \widehat\rho^*x=\widetilde p^*z.$$*


3.  *After choosing the orientation of $E$, one has $$\label{geo:primitive-boundary}
           p_!x=e.$$ With either orientation its coefficient is $\pm1$.*


4.  *The actual negative bundle satisfies $$\label{geo:negative-pontryagin}
           \rho^*p_1(E)=4z.$$

Let $p:S(E)\to B$ and $x$ be as in Proposition <a href="#geo:attachment" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="geo:attachment">19</a>, and let $$\xi=\frac12e(T_{\mathrm{vert}}S(E))\in H^2(S(E);\mathbb Q),
 \qquad X=e^2\in H^4(B;\mathbb Z).$$ There is a class $Y\in H^4(B;\mathbb Q)$ such that, with pullbacks by $p$ suppressed, $$\label{geo:sphere-parameters}
 x=Y+e\xi,\qquad
 \xi^2=p_1(E)/4,\qquad p_!\xi=1,\qquad
 \rho^*Y=z,\qquad 2Y\in H^4(B;\mathbb Z).$$ For an integer $b$, $$\label{geo:Y-lift}
 Y=v+\frac b2e^2.$$ There are rational numbers $\alpha,\beta$ with $$\label{geo:L-parameters}
 L:=e^2p_1(E)/4=\alpha X^2+\beta XY,\qquad
 4\alpha\in\mathbb Z,\qquad |\beta|=1.$$ Here $\beta=1$ for <a href="#geo:incidence-input" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="geo:incidence-input">[geo:incidence-input]</a>; retaining $\beta$ also allows the opposite generator convention, for which $\beta=-1$. Moreover $$\label{geo:two-nilpotents}
 (Y+e\xi)^{r+1}=(Y-e\xi)^{r+1}=0
 \quad\text{in }H^*(S(E);\mathbb Q).$$*


*Count $X,Y$ as polynomial degree one, corresponding to cohomological degree four. For homogeneous polynomials of degree $2r-1$, set $$\ell(P)=\int_B eP(X,Y).$$ Then the middle moment matrix satisfies $$\label{geo:moment-determinant}
 \left|\det\left[
 \ell\big(X^{2r-1-i-j}Y^{i+j}\big)
 \right]_{0\le i,j<r}\right|=r+1,
 \qquad \ell(X^{2r-1})=N.$$ Pullback $p^*:H^*(B;\mathbb Q)\to H^*(S(E);\mathbb Q)$ is injective, and multiplication by $e$ on $H^n(B;\mathbb Q)$ is injective.

For a quaternionic-type Blaschke manifold of dimension $4r$, $r\ge2$, the contact characteristic number of Proposition <a href="#geo:contact" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="geo:contact">17</a> satisfies $$N=\int_{B}e^{4r-1}
 \le \frac1{2r+1}\binom{4r}{2r}.
 \label{alg:sharp-upper}$$
